{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795714185,"nonce":"/X5BqcQXduHoeEandVK40/Of3FI=","message":"The cause given above (the Jacobi-CG that applies M^-1) is not what limits the consistent-mass exponential on the Argyris plate. The limit is the fixed Krylov (Lanczos) size of `jno.solve.exponential()`: `order=40` by default, with no residual or error check, so on a stiff spectrum the matrix exponential action is silently under-resolved and 3-4 digits are lost without a warning.\n\nMeasured on the consistent-mass Argyris case of this issue, relative to an adaptive SDIRK3 reference at rtol 1e-8 (same test, same saved times, CPU, float64):\n\n    order  40   6.8e-4   (the default; the ~7e-4 of the issue)\n    order  60   1.1e-4\n    order  80   2.6e-5\n    order 100   2.0e-6\n\nThe error falls steadily with the order, which is what an under-resolved Krylov space looks like. Replacing the mass CG by a direct solve changed the result not at all (same digits), so the factor-once direct M-solve proposed here would not fix it.\n\nDirection: add an a-posteriori error estimate to the Krylov step (the standard last-column or residual estimate of the Arnoldi/Lanczos exponential) and either grow the order until it meets the march's tolerance or refuse with a message that names `order=`. Until then, a stiff form needs `order=` raised by hand, and the test bound for Argyris (2e-3) records the default, not an accuracy limit of the element. Order 100 is from an earlier run; 40 to 80 were re-run. Not run: orders above 100, and other stiff families.","files":null}]}